-- title:
--   Nonzero divisible subgroup from a coherent p-power torsion tower
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a ring, $M$ an additive commutative group with an $R$-module structure, $p$ a natural number that is prime, and $f : \mathbb{N} \to M$ a sequence subject to two hypotheses: $p \cdot f(n+1) = f(n)$ for every $n$ (the transition relation, with the natural-number scalar action), and $p^n \cdot f(n) = 0$ for every $n$. Let $I \subseteq R$ be a set of scalars such that $t \cdot f(n) = 0$ for every $t \in I$ and every $n$, and assume $f(n) \neq 0$ for at least one $n$. Write $D = \mathrm{AddSubgroup.closure}(\mathrm{range}\, f)$ for the additive subgroup of $M$ generated by the values of $f$. The conclusion is the conjunction of three assertions: $D \neq \bot$, i.e. $D$ is not the trivial subgroup; for every $z \in D$ and every natural number $k > 0$ there exists $z' \in D$ with $k \cdot z' = z$, so $D$ is divisible by every positive integer, with the divisor found inside $D$ itself; and for every $t \in I$ and every $z \in D$ one has $t \cdot z = 0$, so $D$ is annihilated by all of $I$.
--
--   This is the elementary mechanism turning a nonzero coherent sequence of $p^n$-torsion elements (a nonzero element of a $p$-adic Tate module) into a nonzero divisible subgroup of $p$-power torsion annihilated by every scalar annihilating the sequence. It is used in the construction of eigenplanes in Tate modules of modular Jacobians, where the annihilating set $I$ consists of Hecke operators acting through a fixed system of eigenvalues.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AddSubgroup_closure_range_divisible_of_prime_tower.lean

